The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (x->km.seq)
	hlist_del_rcu(&x->byseq);
if (x->id.spi)
	hlist_del_rcu(&x->byspi);
Reason:
The original code used value-based predicates (`x->km.seq` and `x->id.spi`) to decide whether to unhash from the `byseq` and `byspi` lists. These scalar fields can be modified outside `xfrm_state_lock`, creating a race: the deletion may skip removal while the node is still on the list, or attempt removal when it is no longer on the list, leading to corrupted list pointers. Additionally, using `hlist_del_rcu` instead of `hlist_del_init_rcu` means a second deletion (e.g., from a double-free race) writes through a poisoned `pprev`, causing use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
hlist_del_init_rcu(&x->bydst);
hlist_del_init_rcu(&x->bysrc);
if (!hlist_unhashed(&x->byseq))
	hlist_del_init_rcu(&x->byseq);
if (!hlist_unhashed(&x->byspi))
	hlist_del_init_rcu(&x->byspi);
Reason:
The fix uses `hlist_del_init_rcu` for all four hash lists, ensuring that a second deletion is a no-op (since `hlist_unhashed` is true after the first deletion) rather than writing through a poisoned pointer. The condition for unhash is changed from value-based (`x->km.seq`, `x->id.spi`) to list-state-based (`hlist_unhashed`), so the decision correctly tracks whether the node is still on the list, eliminating the race condition.

